## PL/SW Language and File Conventions in the COR24 Ecosystem

### 1. What PL/SW Is

PL/SW (Programming Language for Software Wrighter) is a **PL/I-inspired systems programming language** that compiles to COR24 24-bit RISC assembly (.s files). It is explicitly modeled after IBM's internal PL/S-family languages (PL/S-III, PL/AS, PL/X). It is **not** an assembler -- it is a high-level structured language with a recursive-descent parser, AST, type system, and code generator. However, it provides escape hatches to inline assembly, making it a "systems language" in the classic IBM sense.

The compiler itself is written in C (compiled by tc24r), runs on the COR24 emulator, and reads source via UART.

### 2. File Extension Conventions

| Extension | Purpose | Role |
|-----------|---------|------|
| `.plsw` | PL/SW source code | Main compilation units containing procedures, variables, logic |
| `.msw` | Macro/declaration files | Shared headers: BASED record templates, MACRODEF blocks, %DEFINE constants |
| `.s` | Generated COR24 assembly | Compiler output |

The convention is strict: `.plsw` files are **source modules** that contain executable code (procedures, MAIN). `.msw` files are **include files** that contain definitions only -- BASED record templates, MACRODEF blocks, and %DEFINE constants. They contain no PROC bodies with real logic.

In the build pipeline, `.msw` files are listed first, then the `.plsw` file last:
```bash
./scripts/pipeline.sh include/cvt.msw include/ascb.msw examples/chain.plsw
```

### 3. Constants / %DEFINE Directives

PL/SW uses `%DEFINE` for compile-time constant substitution. These are text-substituted at lex time with zero runtime cost. There is no EQU directive -- `%DEFINE` is the equivalent.

```
%DEFINE UART_DATA 16711936;   /* 0xFF0100 */
%DEFINE NEWLINE 10;
%DEFINE MAX_COUNT 5;
%DEFINE TAG_NULL 0;
%DEFINE TAG_INT 2;
%DEFINE DESC_BYTES 6;
%DEFINE OBK_STRING 1;
%DEFINE OBF_MARK 128;
```

Key syntax points:
- Always terminated with semicolon
- Values are decimal integers (no hex literals in PL/SW -- hex must be written as decimal, e.g., `16711936` for `0xFF0100`)
- Names are substituted wherever they appear in subsequent code
- Defined in `.msw` files for shared use, or at the top of `.plsw` files for module-local use

A special convention: `%DEFINE LIBRARY;` is used to mark a `.plsw` file as a library module (not the entry-point module). This appears in the snobol4 project's non-main modules (`sno_util.plsw`, `sno_lex.plsw`, `sno_exec.plsw`).

### 4. Other Preprocessor Directives

```
%INCLUDE name;          -- Include a .msw file by name (no extension)
%IF condition;          -- Conditional compilation
%DEFINE name value;     -- Compile-time constant
%ELSE;                  -- Conditional else
%ENDIF;                 -- End conditional
```

`%INCLUDE` resolves named files that were registered via the FILE: protocol during compilation. The name is bare (no extension): `%INCLUDE descr;` resolves to `descr.msw`.

### 5. Procedure/Function Structure

Procedures use PL/I syntax:

```
/* Simple procedure */
DELAY: PROC;
    /* body */
END;

/* Procedure with parameters and return value */
ALLOC: PROC(SIZE INT) RETURNS(INT);
    DCL BASE INT;
    BASE = ADDR(ARENA) + ARENA_POS;
    RETURN(BASE);
END;

/* Procedure with multiple parameters */
DESC_INIT: PROC(P PTR, T BYTE, S BYTE, V INT);
    DCL DPTR PTR;
    DPTR = P;
    DPTR->DTAG = T;
END;

/* Naked procedure (pure assembly) */
LED_WRITE: PROC(VAL BYTE);
    ASM DO;
        'la      r0,0xFF0000';
        'lw      r1,9(fp)';
        'sb      r1,0(r0)';
    END;
END;

/* Every .plsw file MUST have a MAIN: PROC */
MAIN: PROC;
    CALL UART_PUTS(ADDR(MSG));
END;
```

Key points:
- Every `.plsw` file must have a `MAIN: PROC; ... END;` -- even library modules include a dummy `MAIN: PROC; END;`
- Procedures are called with `CALL name(args);`
- Parameters are typed inline: `(NAME TYPE, NAME TYPE, ...)`
- Return values via `RETURNS(TYPE)` and `RETURN(expr);`
- Labels use the `NAME: PROC` pattern

### 6. Declaration (DCL) Syntax

```
/* Simple scalars */
DCL LED_STATE BYTE INIT(0);
DCL COUNT INT;
DCL CVTPTR PTR;
DCL X INT(24);           /* Explicit width */

/* Arrays */
DCL MSG(20) CHAR INIT('Hello from PL/SW!');
DCL ARENA(512) BYTE;
DCL SRC(8192) BYTE;
DCL DIGITS(12) CHAR;

/* Static with init */
DCL SPOS INT STATIC INIT(0);
DCL INP_TTY INT STATIC INIT(0);

/* Level-based record structures */
DCL 1 POINT,
    3 X INT,
    3 Y INT;

/* BASED record templates (in .msw files -- no storage allocated) */
DCL 1 DESCR BASED,
    3 DTAG BYTE,
    3 DSUB BYTE,
    3 DRSV BYTE,
    3 DPAY PTR;

/* Nested records */
DCL 1 CVT BASED,
  3 CVTEYE(4) CHAR,
  3 CVTVER    BYTE,
  3 CVTFLAGS  BYTE,
  3 CVTASCBH  PTR,
  3 CVTASCBL  PTR,
  3 CVTCTCB   PTR;
```

Types: `BIT`, `BYTE`, `INT`, `INT(8)`, `INT(16)`, `INT(24)`, `CHAR`, `PTR`/`POINTER`, `WORD`

Storage classes: `STATIC`, `AUTOMATIC` (default for locals), `EXTERNAL`, `BASED`

`BASED` is critical -- it declares a record template that allocates no storage. You access it through a pointer:
```
DCL DPTR PTR;
DPTR = ADDR(D1);
DPTR->DTAG = TAG_INT;    /* Access field via pointer dereference */
```

Record field access:
- Direct: `POINT.X = 100;`
- Via pointer: `P->X`, `CVTPTR->CVTEYE`

### 7. Control Flow

```
/* IF/THEN/ELSE */
IF (N = 0) THEN DO;
    CALL UART_PUTCHAR(48);
    RETURN;
END;

IF (LED_STATE = 0) THEN
    LED_STATE = 1;
ELSE
    LED_STATE = 0;

/* DO WHILE loop */
DO WHILE (N > 0);
    D = N / 10;
    N = D;
END;

/* Counted DO loop */
DO I = 1 TO 10;
    CALL PRINT_INT(I);
END;

/* SELECT/WHEN (switch-case equivalent) */
SELECT;
    WHEN (X = 1) CALL UART_PUTCHAR(65);
    WHEN (X = 2) CALL UART_PUTCHAR(66);
    OTHERWISE CALL UART_PUTCHAR(63);
END;

/* Nested SELECT with DO blocks */
WHEN (X = 4) DO;
    SELECT;
        WHEN (Y = 1) CALL UART_PUTS(ADDR(PA));
        OTHERWISE CALL UART_PUTS(ADDR(QU));
    END;
END;
```

Important: comparison uses single `=` (not `==`), `!=` for not-equal, `AND`/`OR` for logical operators.

### 8. Built-in Functions

- `ADDR(name)` -- address-of operator
- `SIZEOF(name)` -- size of a BASED record template
- `UART_PUTS(addr)` -- print null-terminated string (built into compiler runtime)
- `UART_PUTCHAR(byte)` -- print single character
- `UART_GETCHAR()` -- read single character

### 9. Inline Assembly

```
ASM DO;
    'la      r0,0xFF0000';
    'lw      r1,9(fp)';
    'sb      r1,0(r0)';
END;
```

Each assembly instruction is a string literal (single-quoted) terminated with semicolon. Emitted verbatim into the .s output.

### 10. Macro System (.msw files)

Macros are defined with `MACRODEF` and invoked with `?NAME(...)`:

**Definition (in .msw file):**
```
MACRODEF LED_SET;
    REQUIRED VAL(expr);
    GEN DO;
        'la      r2,16711680';
        'lc      r0,{VAL}';
        'sb      r0,0(r2)';
    END;
END;

MACRODEF DESC_COPY;
    REQUIRED DST(expr); REQUIRED SRC(expr);
    GEN DO;
        'la r1,{SRC}'; 'la r2,{DST}';
        'lb r0,0(r1)'; 'sb r0,0(r2)';
        'lw r0,3(r1)'; 'sw r0,3(r2)';
    END;
END;
```

**Invocation (in .plsw file):**
```
?LED_SET(VAL(0));
?GREET(MSG(_MSG));
?NOP(COUNT(99));
```

Key macro syntax:
- `MACRODEF name; ... END;`
- `REQUIRED param(type);` or `OPTIONAL param(type);`
- Types: `expr`, `lvalue`
- `GEN DO; ... END;` generates assembly with `{PARAM}` substitution
- Invocation: `?NAME(KEYWORD(value), ...);`

### 11. How .msw and .plsw Relate

`.msw` files are **header/include files** containing:
1. `BASED` record templates (struct definitions for pointer access)
2. `%DEFINE` constants
3. `MACRODEF` macro definitions

`.plsw` files are **source modules** containing:
1. `%INCLUDE` directives to pull in .msw definitions
2. Global `DCL` statements (variable declarations with actual storage)
3. `PROC` definitions (actual executable code)
4. A mandatory `MAIN: PROC; ... END;`

The typical file structure of a .plsw module:
```
/* comment header */
%DEFINE LIBRARY;          /* if this is a library module, not the entry point */
%INCLUDE descr;           /* pull in BASED records, %DEFINEs */
%INCLUDE heap;
%INCLUDE snoglob;         /* shared global declarations */

/* Global declarations */
DCL BUFFER(256) BYTE;

/* Procedures */
MY_FUNC: PROC(X INT) RETURNS(INT);
    /* ... */
END;

/* Mandatory MAIN */
MAIN: PROC;
    /* entry point logic, or empty for library modules */
END;
```

### 12. Multi-Module Build (snobol4 project)

The snobol4 project demonstrates the real-world multi-module pattern:

**Module structure:**
- `src/sno_main.plsw` -- entry point (compiled first, provides _start and globals)
- `src/sno_util.plsw` -- utility library (`%DEFINE LIBRARY;` at top)
- `src/sno_lex.plsw` -- lexer/parser library (`%DEFINE LIBRARY;`)
- `src/sno_exec.plsw` -- executor library (`%DEFINE LIBRARY;`)

**Shared state via .msw:**
- `include/snoglob.msw` -- all shared global variable DCLs (included by every module)
- `include/descr.msw` -- descriptor BASED record + %DEFINE constants
- `include/heap.msw` -- heap object BASED records + %DEFINE constants + MACRODEFs
- `include/am.msw` -- bytecode opcode %DEFINE constants
- `include/pat.msw` -- pattern node BASED record + %DEFINE constants
- `include/trace.msw` -- trace %DEFINE constants + MACRODEF

**Build process:** Each module is compiled independently to .s, then a linker tool (`link24` + `meta-gen`) combines the binaries with cross-module symbol resolution.

### 13. Comments

PL/SW uses C-style block comments:
```
/* This is a comment */
/* multi-line
   comment */
```

### 14. Summary of Syntax Conventions

- All statements terminated with semicolon
- Keywords are uppercase: `DCL`, `PROC`, `END`, `IF`, `THEN`, `ELSE`, `DO`, `WHILE`, `TO`, `CALL`, `RETURN`, `RETURNS`, `BASED`, `STATIC`, `INIT`, `ADDR`, `SIZEOF`, `SELECT`, `WHEN`, `OTHERWISE`, `ASM`, `GEN`, `MACRODEF`, `REQUIRED`, `OPTIONAL`
- Identifiers are conventionally uppercase (e.g., `CVTPTR`, `ARENA_POS`, `READ_SRC`)
- Preprocessor directives use `%` prefix: `%DEFINE`, `%INCLUDE`, `%IF`, `%ELSE`, `%ENDIF`
- Macro invocations use `?` prefix: `?LED_SET(VAL(0))`
- String literals use single quotes: `'Hello from PL/SW!'`
- No hex literals -- use decimal equivalents
- Comparison: `=` (equal), `!=` (not equal), `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`
- Logical: `AND`, `OR`
- Arithmetic: `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`
- Pointer dereference: `PTR->FIELD`
- Record field access: `RECORD.FIELD`
- Array indexing: `ARRAY(index)`

